## Previous Critical #1 — project-create idempotency: RESOLVED

Independent trace of `productionFetch` (`/create`, `/reconcile`) plus Durable Object methods:

- There is exactly one project-create POST site in the whole runtime (`production.ts:314`). The legacy POC create handler (`index.ts:507 createProject`) is dead code: both `/poc/create` and `POST /poc/projects` return 410 and nothing calls it.
- `reserveCreate` mints a fresh 128-bit nonce with a 90s lease and increments `createAttempts`; it is gated by `createReservationAllowed(state, createAttempts)` = `state ∈ {organization_selected, project_retry_authorized} && createAttempts < 2`, so at most two create POSTs can ever be issued for one transaction.
- `project_creating → project_reconciliation_required` is only reachable through `claimCreateReconciliation` (requires `operationLeaseActive === false`) or `createUncertain` (requires the active create nonce). An active lease therefore cannot be converted into retry permission by a second request; the route converts the failure into 409 `operation_in_progress` and never continues to the POST (`production.ts:302-304`).
- A zero-match reconciliation cannot authorize retry while a create may be live: to reach `reconciliationAbsent` the state must already be `project_reconciliation_required`, which requires an expired lease, and the Worker's own create POST is bounded by `AbortSignal.timeout(20_000)` — well inside the 90s lease. Zero-match also does not auto-POST in the same request: it returns `project_retry_authorized` and requires a further `/create` call (`production.ts:305-313`).
- Stale responses cannot mutate newer state: `recordProject` and `createUncertain` both require `state === "project_creating"` and `operation.nonce === n`; a newer attempt always replaces the nonce, so the old request's commit fails closed (`stale_operation`), and `project_creating` and `project_retry_authorized` cannot transition directly back into a POST-capable state without a new reservation.
- Multiple matches during reconciliation become terminal `project_identity_ambiguous` (409); inconclusive reconciliation leaves `project_reconciliation_required` unchanged and returns 502 with no POST.
- Organization/project identity cannot change while reconciliation is unresolved: `selectOrganization` requires `authorization_pending`, and the create body uses the DO-stored organization slug and requested project name, never client-supplied values.

## Previous Critical #2 — OAuth expiry / token resurrection: RESOLVED

- Callback claim is one-time and state-bound: `oauthCallback` hashes the presented state, requires `authorization_pending`, `!oauthStateUsed`, a matching stored SHA-256 state hash, and a live TTL; it sets `oauthStateUsed` and returns the PKCE verifier. Replay returns null → 400.
- Credential persistence is race-free at the final step: `saveOAuthFromCallback` performs hashing and AES-GCM sealing first, then reloads durable state, expires it if the TTL has passed, and evaluates `oauthCredentialSaveAllowed(state, used, storedHash, providedHash, txExpiresAt, tokenExpiresAt, now)` immediately before a synchronous single-row SQLite write. There is no interleavable await between the final authoritative check and the durable write, and ciphertext is never written outside that validated block.
- Delayed callbacks after TTL cannot resurrect credentials: expiry clears `oauthStateHash`, `oauthStateUsed`-independent state becomes `expired`, and the TTL predicate fails, so the DO throws and nothing is persisted. The same holds after the alarm has run, and also when the alarm has not yet run but `Date.now() >= expiresAt`.
- Ready/terminal transactions cannot receive credentials: the predicate requires `authorization_pending`; `terminal_error`/`ready`/`expired` all fail closed. `create` cannot overwrite an existing record.

## Previous Important #1 — verification tri-state: RESOLVED

`runFixedVerification` returns `passed` only when the fixed SQL returns all 12 named checks as `true`; `assertion_failed` only when all 12 are present as booleans and at least one is `false`; otherwise `indeterminate`. Non-2xx (including 429 and 5xx), thrown/aborted fetches, and malformed/unusable response shapes are all `indeterminate`. `finishVerification` maps only `assertion_failed` to terminal `verification_failed`; `indeterminate` keeps the transaction in `verifying` (retryable, 202), and only `passed` reaches `ready`.

## Previous Important #2 — migration/verification operation ownership: RESOLVED

`claimOperation` stores a durable operation record (kind, nonce, startedAt, lease) under an atomic read-check-write with no await between load and save. An active lease blocks duplicates: verification duplicates (and migration duplicates while `migrating` with a live lease) throw `operation_in_progress` → 409. A stale migration lease is first moved to `migration_reconciliation_required` and the call is rejected without any POST. `finishMigration`/`finishVerification` require current state, matching operation kind, and the exact nonce, so stale completions cannot advance state, cannot delete the current credentials, and cannot mark a transaction ready. Retry after lease expiry always mints a fresh nonce.

## Previous Important #3 — migration recovery: RESOLVED

`migration_reconciliation_required` is reachable from the real route and is genuinely resumable: `claimOperation("migration")` accepts it (and `project_waiting`), moves to `migrating`, and `runCanonicalMigrations` always reads official Supabase migration history first and applies only the missing canonical suffix. Every migration execution path therefore reconciles history before any POST; an ambiguous previous outcome can never be blindly resubmitted. Divergent/unexpected history, off-bundle SQL digests, and post-POST history that does not advance all fail closed (terminal `migration_history_mismatch`, or `indeterminate → migration_reconciliation_required` for transport failures). Cross-stage confusion is prevented by the transition table (`project_reconciliation_required → migrating` is illegal) and by `claimOperation` state gating.

## Project readiness

`/migrate` checks Management project status before touching migrations and only proceeds on `ACTIVE_HEALTHY`. Any other status, non-2xx (429/5xx), malformed body, or thrown/aborted fetch yields `ready() === false`, which maps to `finishMigration(..., indeterminate)` → retryable `migration_reconciliation_required` (HTTP 202). A newly created but not-yet-healthy project is therefore never treated as migration drift, never terminal, and never triggers replacement-project creation. Retries are bounded by the shared expensive-operation cap and TTL expiry; no infinite polling.

